Compare laboratories, reference values, and degrees of agreement (@met-interlab: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @metrology, #source "../sources/International-Comparison.lingua#r_3VVRRP0PY4YGAGTB7M22VNGDJ1", #source "../sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN", #source "../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E", #metrology "Conformity-and-risk.lingua#r_41AJHYZ4Q8B821Z892PNNK1MEJ") { r_4B3WR8EBCNJRGVMR6HCG66Y1S9
**Read:** [CIPM MRA, CMCs, and key comparisons — BIPM](../sources/International-Comparison.lingua#r_3VVRRP0PY4YGAGTB7M22VNGDJ1), one complete actual comparison report, with its identifier and date retained; [Measurement Process Characterization — NIST/SEMATECH](../sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN), comparison and measurement-process material; [Simple Guide for Evaluating and Expressing Uncertainty — Possolo](../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E), a multi-source or comparison-based example.

A comparison needs a common measurand, compatible conditions, an adequately stable item or material, a protocol, and a model for the reference value and participant results. Transport, sample heterogeneity, drift, and shared traceability can affect the apparent differences. Agreement is meaningful only after these relationships are understood.

Distinguish key or supplementary comparisons, proficiency testing, bilateral comparisons, and an informal duplicate measurement. Their purposes and statistical interpretations differ. A consensus mean can be appropriate under one model and misleading when common effects, heterogeneous uncertainty, or a biased cluster dominate.

For compatible independent expanded uncertainties using the same coverage convention, E_n = (x − x_ref)/√(U_x² + U_ref²) is a common normalized comparison statistic. Dependence requires the corresponding covariance treatment, and a reference value estimated using the participant can create dependence. A z score using a proficiency standard deviation answers a different question from a statistic based on reported measurement uncertainties.

**Work:** calculate E_n for a teaching difference of 0.6 units with U_x = 0.4 and U_ref = 0.5 units; the value is about 0.937 under these assumptions. Then introduce shared reference information and explain why the denominator must be reconsidered. Interpret a compatibility criterion only under the actual protocol, not as a universal proof of accuracy.

Read a full relevant KCDB comparison report and one handbook or technical-guide comparison case. Reconstruct the reference-value method, uncertainty, participant covariance where applicable, and conclusion. Discuss a participant that reports a much larger uncertainty and one with a small but inconsistent result. The purpose is to examine evidence and improve methods rather than reward a particular score in isolation.

